| <!doctype html>
html [lang=en]
  head
    meta [charset=utf-8]
    meta [name=viewport content=width=device-width,initial-scale=1]
    title NanoC: less syntax, full control
    meta [name=description content="NanoC is a compact, self-hosted language that generates plain C11 or C++17. Guide, examples, and compiler stats."]
    link [rel=icon href=favicon.svg type=image/svg+xml]
    link [rel=stylesheet href=style.css]
    link [rel=stylesheet href=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/prismjs@1.29.0/themes/prism-tomorrow.min.css]
    script [src=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/prismjs@1.29.0/components/prism-core.min.js defer] 
    script [src=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/prismjs@1.29.0/components/prism-clike.min.js defer] 
    script [src=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/prismjs@1.29.0/components/prism-c.min.js defer] 
    script [src=https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/prismjs@1.29.0/components/prism-bash.min.js defer] 
    script [src=app.js defer] 
  body#top
    | {{nav}}
    main
      section.hero.wrap [style=border:0;padding-bottom:70px]
        div
          p.kicker C, with less ceremony
          h1 Write closer<br>to the <em>idea.</em>
          p.lede A compact, self-hosted language that generates plain C11 or C++17. Indented blocks, macros that run, compile-time and build-time code, inline assembly.
          div.cta <a class="btn solid" href="play.html">Try it in your browser</a><a class="btn" href="#guide">Start the guide</a><a class="btn" href="#stats">See the numbers</a>
        div.hero-code.boxed
          pre
            code.language-nanoc
              | @f clamp(int x,lo,hi) int
              |   if x&lt;lo
              |     lo
              |   elif x&gt;hi
              |     hi
              |   else
              |     x
              | @f main int
              |   total:=0
              |   for i 0..10
              |     total+=clamp(i,2,7)
              |   total!=45
          span.badge [data-stat=badge] −7% lines vs C
      section#stats.wrap
        p.kicker Measured on the compiler itself
        h2 Compact source.<br><em>Less to read.</em>
        p.sub NanoC's own compiler is written in NanoC. Every source file is run through <code>nanoc</code>, and the C it generates is counted against the NanoC it came from.
        div.stat-grid
          div.stat <b class="hi" data-stat="lines-saved">–</b><span>fewer lines than the C it generates</span>
          div.stat <b data-stat="bytes-saved">–</b><span>fewer bytes of source</span>
          div.stat <b data-stat="nc-lines">–</b><span>NanoC lines across <em data-stat="files">–</em> files</span>
          div.stat <b data-stat="c-lines">–</b><span>lines of C it expands to</span>
        div.compare
          div.legend <span><i style="background:var(--lime)"></i>NanoC</span><span><i style="background:#3a3c33"></i>Generated C</span>
          div#areas
          p.method Method: non-blank lines and bytes, per area, summed over <code>src/**/*.nc</code>. Generated by <code>tools/site/site.nc</code> and updated on each regeneration (<span data-stat="generated">–</span>).
      section#vs.wrap
        p.kicker How it compares
        h2 Not a new world.<br><em>A better door into C.</em>
        p.sub The table is hand-written; the numbers below it are measured by compiling every example in <code>examples/</code> with this build of NanoC.
        div [style=margin-top:44px] {{lang-table}}
        h3.h3 Measured per example
        | {{ex-table}}
        p.method Method: non-blank lines and bytes of each example against the C the compiler emits. Generated at site build time ({{built}}). <a href="compare.html">Read them side by side →</a>
      section#guide.wrap
        p.kicker Language guide
        h2 Learn it in <em>ten minutes.</em>
        p.sub Every example compiles and runs with the current compiler. Unmarked C stays valid, so you can adopt NanoC one function at a time.
        div.guide [style=margin-top:56px]
          nav.toc [aria-label="Guide sections"] <a href="#g1"><span>01</span>Hello</a> <a href="#g2"><span>02</span>Functions</a> <a href="#g3"><span>03</span>Values</a> <a href="#g4"><span>04</span>Locals &amp; loops</a> <a href="#g5"><span>05</span>Macros</a> <a href="#g6"><span>06</span>Compile time</a> <a href="#g7"><span>07</span>Build time</a> <a href="#g8"><span>08</span>Assembly</a> <a href="#g9"><span>09</span>C++ &amp; C interop</a> <a href="#g10"><span>10</span>Cheat sheet</a>
          div
            article#g1.lesson
              div.n 01 · START
              h3 Hello, NanoC
              p Save this as <code>hello.nc</code>. Lines starting with <code>#</code> pass through unchanged, so any C header works.
              pre
                code.language-nanoc
                  | #include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
                  | @f main int
                  |   puts("Hello from NanoC!")
                  |   0
              p.note [style=margin-top:16px] Build: <code>nanoc hello.nc -o hello.c &amp;&amp; cc hello.c -o hello &amp;&amp; ./hello</code>
            article#g2.lesson
              div.n 02 · FUNCTIONS
              h3 Bare return types
              p The return type goes after the parameters, with no arrow. Omit it for <code>void</code>. Group parameters that share a type with a comma. A one-line body can be an <code>=</code> expression.
              pre
                code.language-nanoc
                  | #include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
                  | @f add(int a,b) int = a+b
                  | @f greet(char *name)
                  |   printf("hi %s\n",name)
                  | @f main int
                  |   greet("NanoC")
                  |   printf("%d\n",add(2,3))
                  |   0
              ul
                li An indented body returns its final expression. No <code>return</code> needed.
                li No braces, no semicolons, no parentheses around conditions.
            article#g3.lesson
              div.n 03 · VALUES
              h3 Blocks produce values
              p An <code>if</code>/<code>elif</code>/<code>else</code> that covers every branch returns the value of whichever branch runs.
              pre
                code.language-nanoc
                  | @f sign(int x) int
                  |   if x&lt;0
                  |     -1
                  |   elif x&gt;0
                  |     1
                  |   else
                  |     0
                  | @f main int = sign(-9)!=-1
            article#g4.lesson
              div.n 04 · LOCALS &amp; LOOPS
              h3 Inference and ranges
              p <code>:=</code> infers the type of a scalar literal. <code>for i 1..6</code> counts from 1 up to but not including 6.
              pre
                code.language-nanoc
                  | #include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
                  | @f main int
                  |   total:=0
                  |   for i 1..6
                  |     total+=i
                  |   printf("%d\n",total)
                  |   0
              ul
                li Spell the type when you need it: <code>int x=call()</code>.
                li Typed range: <code>for i:size_t 0..n</code>. Bounds are evaluated once.
                li Plain C loops still work: <code>for int i=0;i&lt;n;i++</code>.
                li C++ iterates containers: <code>for value values</code>.
            article#g5.lesson
              div.n 05 · MACROS
              h3 Token templates
              p <code>@m</code> defines a template. <code>$x</code> captures a token. A macro with <code>=</code> and no parameters is a named constant.
              pre
                code.language-nanoc
                  | #include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
                  | @m twice($x)
                  |   (($x)*2)
                  | @m answer = 42
                  | @m maxfn($name,$type)
                  |   @f $name($type a,b) $type = a&gt;b?a:b
                  | @maxfn(max_int,int)
                  | @f main int
                  |   printf("%d %d\n",@twice(@answer),max_int(2,3))
                  |   0
              p.note [style=margin-top:16px] Use <code>$fresh(tmp)</code> for generated names that must not collide. <code>$$</code> writes a literal <code>$</code>.
            article#g6.lesson
              div.n 06 · COMPILE TIME
              h3 Generate code with <code>@ct</code>
